              "pdf_page": 1288,
              "printed_page": 1206,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": "•Cats:"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Hemorrhage into the cranial vault,",
              "pdf_page": 1288,
              "printed_page": 1206,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": "•Cats:"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "gastrointestinal tract, subcutaneous space,",
              "pdf_page": 1288,
              "printed_page": 1206,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": "•Cats:"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "intramuscular space, and urinary tract is less",
              "pdf_page": 1288,
              "printed_page": 1206,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": "•Cats:"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "common.",
              "pdf_page": 1288,
              "printed_page": 1206,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": "•Cats:"
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    "TREATMENT": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "is preferred due to the potential for limited gastrointestinal absorption. Use a small needle in multiple locations, if needed, and monitor for anaphylactoid reaction.",
          "pdf_page": 1289,
          "printed_page": 1207,
          "column": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Treat acute hemorrhagic shock with IV crystalloids, colloids, or blood products as needed. Initial crystalloid boluses of 10–20 mL/kg can be given over 15–20 minutes during stabilization. Repeat 2–3 times as needed to increase blood pressure.",
          "pdf_page": 1289,
          "printed_page": 1207,
          "column": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Packed RBCs, whole blood, fresh frozen plasma, or frozen plasma may be necessary to replace coagulation factors.",
          "pdf_page": 1289,
          "printed_page": 1207,
          "column": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Treat life-threatening cardiac tamponade or hemothorax with pericardiocentesis or thoracocentesis as indicated. If the patient is stable and responding to treatment, these procedures should be avoided. With time, the blood will reabsorb.",
          "pdf_page": 1289,
          "printed_page": 1207,
          "column": 1
        }
      ],
      "subsections": {
        "GENERAL": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "GENERAL",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "APPROPRIATE HEALTH CARE": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "APPROPRIATE HEALTH CARE",
          "canonical_heading": "APPROPRIATE HEALTH CARE",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "cytopenia.",
              "pdf_page": 1288,
              "printed_page": 1206,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Patients in acute crisis should be",
              "pdf_page": 1288,
              "printed_page": 1206,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Coagulation factor deficiency secondary hospitalized. to hepatic disease—assay for factor",
              "pdf_page": 1288,
              "printed_page": 1206,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Patients without active hemorrhaging or a deficiencies (e.g., factor VII), liver function, stabilized coagulopathy may be treated on an bile acids. outpatient basis.",
              "pdf_page": 1288,
              "printed_page": 1206,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Disseminated intravascular coagulopathy",
              "pdf_page": 1288,
              "printed_page": 1206,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Vitamin K1 is antidotal at 2.5–5 mg/kg which is typically associated with neoplasia, q24h or divided q12h. The preferred route of sepsis, pancreatitis, or concurrent disease. Lab administration is oral; however, in case of a evidence includes prolonged prothrombin coagulopathic patient, the subcutaneous route time (PT), partial thromboplastin time",
              "pdf_page": 1288,
              "printed_page": 1206,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "NURSING CARE": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "NURSING CARE",
          "canonical_heading": "NURSING CARE",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Provide supportive care as needed.",
              "pdf_page": 1289,
              "printed_page": 1207,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "ACTIVITY": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "ACTIVITY",
          "canonical_heading": "ACTIVITY",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Confine the patient until the coagulopathy is stabilized as activity enhances blood loss.",
              "pdf_page": 1289,
              "printed_page": 1207,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "DIET": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DIET",
          "canonical_heading": "DIET",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Feed a nutritious, high-quality protein diet to support coagulation factor synthesis.",
              "pdf_page": 1289,
              "printed_page": 1207,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "CLIENT EDUCATION": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "CLIENT EDUCATION",
          "canonical_heading": "CLIENT EDUCATION",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Warn client that reexposure, even months to years later, could be a serious problem due to persistence of second-generation anticoagulants in the liver.",
              "pdf_page": 1289,
              "printed_page": 1207,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Encourage the owner to pick up all loose/unprotected bait on the property.",
              "pdf_page": 1289,
              "printed_page": 1207,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Do not discontinue vitamin K1 therapy early, even if the patient is asymptomatic.",
              "pdf_page": 1289,
              "printed_page": 1207,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "SURGICAL CONSIDERATIONS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SURGICAL CONSIDERATIONS",
          "canonical_heading": "SURGICAL CONSIDERATIONS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Thoracentesis may be important for removing free thoracic blood, which causes dyspnea and respiratory failure (see Appropriate Health Care).",
              "pdf_page": 1289,
              "printed_page": 1207,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "The coagulopathy must be corrected before",
              "pdf_page": 1289,
              "printed_page": 1207,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "MEDICAL": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "MEDICAL",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "SURGERY": {
