        "PREGNANCY/FERTILITY/BREEDING": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "PREGNANCY/FERTILITY/BREEDING",
          "canonical_heading": "PREGNANCY/FERTILITY/BREEDING",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Warfarin, diphacinone, and perhaps other anticoagulant rodenticides may pass into amniotic fluid and to the fetuses of an exposed pregnant animal. These toxicants may also pass via the milk and present concerns for nursing offspring. In general, antidotal treatment of the pregnant or nursing patient is recommended. Alternatively, nursing young may be weaned.",
              "pdf_page": 1289,
              "printed_page": 1207,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Pregnant animals can abort due to placental hemorrhage and detachment.",
              "pdf_page": 1289,
              "printed_page": 1207,
              "column": 3
            }
          ]
        },
